### Fixing method.childParameterType

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/blob/2.2.x/website/errors/method.childParameterType.md

Shows the necessary adjustment to widen the child method's parameter type to match the parent's signature.

```diff-php
 <?php declare(strict_types = 1);

 class ChildClass extends ParentClass
 {
-	public function process(string $value): void
+	public function process(string|int $value): void
 	{
 	}
 }
```

--------------------------------

### Example of method.childParameterType violation

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/blob/2.2.x/website/errors/method.childParameterType.md

Demonstrates a child class narrowing a parameter type from string|int to string, which causes a type mismatch.

```php
<?php declare(strict_types = 1);

class ParentClass
{
	public function process(string|int $value): void
	{
	}
}

class ChildClass extends ParentClass
{
	public function process(string $value): void
	{
	}
}
```

--------------------------------

### Add Native Type Declaration to Parameter

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/blob/2.2.x/website/errors/missingType.parameter.md

Fixes the missing type declaration by adding `mixed` as the parameter type. This ensures PHPStan can perform more accurate type checking.

```php
<?php declare(strict_types = 1);

function processData(mixed $value): bool
{
	return $value !== null;
}
```

### method.childParameterType

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/blob/2.2.x/website/errors/method.childParameterType.md

A child class overrides a method from its parent, but the parameter type in the child is narrower than in the parent. This violates the Liskov Substitution Principle: code that works with the parent type should also work with any child type. For example, if a parent method accepts `string|int`, but a child method only accepts `string`, code passing an `int` would break.

--------------------------------

### missingType.parameter > Why is it reported?

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/blob/2.2.x/website/errors/missingType.parameter.md

The missingType.parameter error occurs when a function or method parameter lacks a type declaration. Without an explicit type, PHPStan defaults to treating the parameter as mixed. This limitation prevents the static analyzer from performing accurate type checking on the parameter's usage within the function or validating the values provided by callers.

